# earned_trust.ever — the same answer, at five different prices.
#
# Every value in Ever carries how far it is believed, out of 256. The
# execute floor is 128: below it the language has given you a number but
# not permission to act on it. This program's answer never changes. What
# changes is what the answer is worth, and you change that by supplying
# evidence from OUTSIDE the program — which is the whole idea. Evidence
# is not something source code gets to assert about itself.
#
# Compound growth at 10% a period. `n` strictly decreases in the
# self-call, so the semantic pass can prove this terminates — which is
# what makes it eligible to be anchored, further down.

def growth(p, n) = if n <= 0 then p else growth(p * 11 / 10, n - 1)

def main() = growth(100, 3)

# ── evidence earns confidence ─────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Every line below was run to produce the number beside it.
#
#   $ ezrun.py examples/earned_trust.ever --call 'growth(100, 3)'
#   133.1  @ 120/256
#
# 120 is [DEF]: a definition is a claim, not a verification, and it
# enters below the floor. Nothing has checked this code. Now supply
# witnesses — cases you claim it already gets right:
#
#   ... --call 'growth(100, 3)' -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100'
#   133.1  @ 120/256        one witness is not yet enough
#
#   ... -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100' -x 'growth(100, 1) = 110'
#   133.1  @ 183/256        two hold, and 183 clears the floor
#
#   ... -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100' -x 'growth(100, 1) = 110' \
#       -x 'growth(100, 2) = 121'
#   133.1  @ 217/256        three hold
#
# Be wrong about one and the language notices, without being told which:
#
#   ... -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100' -x 'growth(100, 1) = 110' \
#       -x 'growth(100, 2) = 999'
#   133.1  @ 122/256        two of three held, and 122 is back below
#
# ── what you cannot do ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# State the same case three times and you have stated one witness three
# times, not three witnesses. Corroboration creates nothing:
#
#   ... -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100' -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100' \
#       -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100'
#   133.1  @ 120/256
#
# Give one case two different answers and the evidence is refused
# outright, because evidence that contradicts itself is not evidence:
#
#   ... -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100' -x 'growth(100, 0) = 999'
#   ezrun: --example 'growth(100, 0) = 999': growth(100, 0) was already
#          given 100 as its answer. ...                        [exit 2]
#
# And evidence for a callee does not launder an unverified caller. Run
# the program through main() with growth fully evidenced and the answer
# is still capped, because main() is the thing that produced it and
# nobody has checked main():
#
#   $ ezrun.py examples/earned_trust.ever \
#       -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100' -x 'growth(100, 1) = 110' \
#       -x 'growth(100, 2) = 121'
#   133.1  @ 120/256
#
# That is [APP] — min(c_f, c_args, c_result) — doing exactly its job.
#
# ── and then earn depth ───────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Unanchored recursion stops at floor(pi) = 3. Twenty periods is past it:
#
#   $ ezrun.py examples/earned_trust.ever -d 3 --call 'growth(100, 20)'
#   Z(unbounded) — depth ceiling 3 exceeded                    [exit 1]
#
# Anchoring lifts the ceiling, and is refused twice over: once if the
# function has not cleared the execute floor, and once if no parameter
# can be shown to decrease. Confidence proves trust, not termination —
# a function can sit at 240/256 and still loop forever.
#
#   $ ezrun.py examples/earned_trust.ever -d 3 --call 'growth(100, 20)' \
#       -x 'growth(100, 0) = 100' -x 'growth(100, 1) = 110' \
#       -x 'growth(100, 2) = 121' -a growth
#   672.7499949325598  @ 217/256
#
# ── both runners ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# This program sits in the subset the two lineages share, so the Java
# runtime — a separate implementation with no shared code — agrees:
#
#   $ 5-runtime-java/ezr examples/earned_trust.ever
#   133.1  @ 120/256
